use std
into rotor_gpu.amni

This file holds GPU kernels (Helium `gpu fn`, which hc turns into WebGPU and Vulkan compute shaders and which also run on the CPU as plain functions) for the powered rotorcraft lattice Boltzmann solver whose setup, rendering and CPU path live in rotor_lbm.gold. It has no `main()`. Each kernel's first parameter n is the cell index (one invocation per cell). All math is f32. Grids are NX by NY by NZ cells, N = NX·NY·NZ, cell index c = x plus NX·(y plus NY·z) with cell center (x plus 0.5, y plus 0.5, z plus 0.5). Distributions are stored as entry i·N plus c for the 19 directions.

Shared buffers. `sol` (u32) packs one part label byte per cell, four cells per entry: 0 is fluid. The helper `lab(sol, c)` (sol a read-only u32 slice, c a u32 cell index) returns the label of cell c: (sol[c / 4] shifted right by 8·(c mod 4)) and 255. Both kernels read every label through `lab`, passing their `sol` buffer. `tb` (i32, 76 entries) holds the lattice tables: entries 0 to 18 are the x directions, 19 to 37 the y directions, 38 to 56 the z directions and 57 to 75 the opposite direction of each. `fq` (f32, 38 entries) holds the 19 lattice weights then the 19 far-field equilibrium values.

The helper `feq(ex, ey, ez, w, r, ux, uy, uz)` (three i32 directions and five f32) returns, with eu the dot product of the direction (each converted to f32) with (ux, uy, uz): w·r·(1 plus 3·eu plus 4.5·eu·eu minus 1.5·(ux·ux plus uy·uy plus uz·uz)).

`rg_step` is a GPU kernel (a Helium gpu fn) whose parameters are, in order: n, NX, NY, NZ, OX and acc (u32), tau and cs (f32), then the slices sol (read-only u32), tb (read-only i32), fq (read-only f32), fa (read-only f32), fb (writable f32), frc (read-only f32), wv (read-only f32) and avg (writable f32). It advances cell n by one step. It returns at once when n is at least N or the cell's label is not 0. x, y and z are the cell coordinates as i32. f is a local array of 19 f32, nw starts false. For each direction i (ex, ey, ez from `tb`): sx is x minus ex, sy is y minus ey, sz is z minus ez. The outlet value of direction i is `feq` of direction i with weight fq[i], density 1 and velocity (bx, by, bz) (an ambient-pressure open boundary). When sx is below 0, sy is below 0, sy is at least NY or sz is at least NZ, f[i] is fq[19 plus i] (still-air far field). Else when sx is at least NX: f[i] is fa[i·N plus n] when OX is 1, otherwise fq[19 plus i]. Else when sz is below 0, f[i] is the outlet value. bx, by and bz are worked out once before the direction loop and only when z is 0 (otherwise they are 0): the sums over j of fa[j·N plus n] times the x, y and z directions of j (as f32), each divided by the sum over j of fa[j·N plus n], then all three scaled to length 0.3 when their length is above 0.3. Else with s the index of (sx, sy, sz): when the label of s is 0, f[i] is fa[i·N plus s]; otherwise o is tb[57 plus i], fo is fa[o·N plus n], f[i] is fo plus 6·fq[i]·(ex·wv[3s] plus ey·wv[3s plus 1] plus ez·wv[3s plus 2]) and nw becomes true. Then r is the sum of f, and mx, my, mz the sums of f[i] times its x, y, z direction. Fx, Fy, Fz are frc[3n], frc[3n plus 1], frc[3n plus 2]. ux is (mx plus 0.5·Fx)/r, uy and uz the same; when the length of (ux, uy, uz) is above 0.3 all three are scaled to length 0.3. The six stress sums qxx, qyy, qzz, qxy, qxz, qyz add, for each i, d = f[i] minus `feq` of direction i with weight fq[i], density r and velocity (ux, uy, uz), times the matching products of the direction components. Q is the square root of (qxx² plus qyy² plus qzz² plus 2·(qxy² plus qxz² plus qyz²)); w2 is 0.5 when nw is true, otherwise 1; te is 0.5·(tau plus the square root of (tau·tau plus 18·1.4142135·cs·cs·w2·Q/r)); vx is ux plus 0.5·Fx/r (the regularized collision keeps no first-order non-equilibrium part, so this shift alone must carry the second half step of force), vy and vz the same with Fy and Fz; om is 1 minus 1/te; t3 is 1/3. For each i with ex, ey, ez its directions as f32: fb[i·N plus n] becomes `feq` of direction i with weight fq[i], density r and velocity (vx, vy, vz), plus om·fq[i]·4.5·((ex·ex minus t3)·qxx plus (ey·ey minus t3)·qyy plus (ez·ez minus t3)·qzz plus 2·(ex·ey·qxy plus ex·ez·qxz plus ey·ez·qyz)). When acc is 1, avg[4n], avg[4n plus 1], avg[4n plus 2] and avg[4n plus 3] get r, ux, uy and uz added.

`rg_wall` is a GPU kernel (a Helium gpu fn) whose parameters are, in order: n, NX, NY and NZ (u32), then the slices sol (read-only u32), tb (read-only i32), fq (read-only f32), fa (read-only f32), wv (read-only f32) and wf (writable f32). It adds the force the fluid puts on solid cell n during the step that streams from fa (the still-air pressure taken out). It returns at once when n is at least N or the cell's label is 0. With x, y, z the coordinates of n as i32, gx, gy, gz start at 0. For each direction i from 1 to 18 (ex, ey, ez from `tb`): the neighbor is (x plus ex, y plus ey, z plus ez); it is skipped unless it is inside the grid and its label is 0; with c its index and o = tb[57 plus i], fo is fa[o·N plus c] and fw is fo plus 6·fq[i]·(ex·wv[3n] plus ey·wv[3n plus 1] plus ez·wv[3n plus 2]); p = fo plus fw minus 2·fq[i]; gx, gy, gz get p times the x, y, z direction of o added. Then wf[3n], wf[3n plus 1], wf[3n plus 2] get gx, gy, gz added.
